WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:02.930
- [Interviewer] All right, Amisha.

00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:03.763
- Yes

00:00:03.763 --> 00:00:04.596
- [Interviewer] Amisha,
where'd you grow up?

00:00:04.596 --> 00:00:05.429
Where are you from originally?

00:00:05.429 --> 00:00:08.120
- I'm originally from Belize,

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:11.240
moved out here to the
States started in Atkinson

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:14.560
and then moved to Long Beach
with my mom and my dad.

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:17.430
They were married, he
was in and out of prison,

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:20.320
so then she turned out to have parties

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.160
and drinking and smoking and fighting,

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.563
and me, my sister and my brother
all have different fathers.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:30.400
I would say I'm the black sheep,

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.610
because I was always
the one getting beat on,

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:36.740
pulled by my hair, hit
with arms, broomsticks,

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:38.673
having carpet burns,

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:42.710
black eyes and that all
started with my mother.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:44.270
I had to take care of my little sister

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:45.470
and my little brother.

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:47.980
At six years old I learned how to cook,

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:49.710
I learned how to change diapers,

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:53.250
I learned how to potty train a boy,

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.460
because my mom was never home.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:56.850
- [Interviewer] Where was mom?

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:00.690
- Running the streets,
probably prostituting.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.700
I know she had her head busted one time

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:05.820
and she came home with
a shower cap on her head

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:08.040
and had blood all in it.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.300
I don't know if the guy
was trying to rape her

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.590
or rob her or she was
probably trying to rob him,

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:12.423
I don't know.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:15.540
So when I would get up and go to school,

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:19.720
I would fix their food, I would
pack my sister's diaper bag

00:01:20.639 --> 00:01:22.110
and send them next door,

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.460
but the next door neighbor
wasn't hearing that.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.470
I pretty much grew up around
a whole bunch of cats.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.040
Father got out of prison, came got me,

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:34.790
brought me to California, Los Angeles,

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:36.670
with his side of the family.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.830
Needless to say, same
events, I was a black sheep,

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:41.710
I was always in trouble,
I was ditching school,

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:46.380
I didn't listen, I had a C
average, my cousin was A average.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.900
She went to the magnet
school and I was worthless

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.150
because, that's just
not what I wanted to do.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:55.970
He went back to prison,

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:59.080
so I was stuck with my
grandma and my auntie,

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.120
and then I hadn't seen my mom for years.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.270
I didn't see my mom again
until I was about maybe 14

00:02:07.537 --> 00:02:08.370
and I tried to talk to her

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:10.840
about her situation with me and her,

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.150
and she told me that I was lying.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:15.490
Mind you, at six years old I got raped,

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:16.323
three times.

00:02:16.323 --> 00:02:17.870
The first time I told my mother

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:20.513
she called me a lying
bitch and slapped me.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.140
So as a result, I never said anything else

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:26.160
and it happened two more times.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.863
At the age of 14, I got raped
and then at the age of 16,

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:35.970
I got raped by three men,
back to back for seven days,

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.800
held at gunpoint in Detroit,

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.140
they kidnapped me from Inglewood
and they knocked me out

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:45.930
and I woke up in a car bouncing,

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:47.900
I had peed on myself and everything

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:49.963
and the next thing I
know I was in Detroit.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:54.610
I had my jaw broken, I been
stabbed with an ice pick,

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:57.100
I've been robbed, I've
been held at gunpoint,

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:58.920
I've been slammed up against a brick wall,

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:02.410
I've been in a car accident
where I almost broke my spine,

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:07.070
I left home when I was 16, I've
been down here on Skid Row,

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:09.560
I done gotten to it with other men,

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:14.550
I done got cut down here,
I have bruises all over me,

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:16.560
I have a big burn mark on my leg

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.643
for where a man tried to set me on fire.

00:03:22.529 --> 00:03:24.510
I mean, I don't know, I
just been through a lot.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:25.343
I've been though a lot.

00:03:25.343 --> 00:03:28.120
My daddy died when I was 17,

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.530
which in turn means in all actuality,

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:32.320
I have four years with him
out of my whole entire life.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:35.288
- [Interviewer] So you were
on your own since when?

00:03:35.288 --> 00:03:36.756
- All through it, didn't have nobody.

00:03:36.756 --> 00:03:37.589
- [Interviewer] No, but--

00:03:37.589 --> 00:03:38.990
- His mother died 2000.

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:43.450
He died 2001 and my
mother's mother died 2002.

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:44.760
- [Interviewer] So about when you were 16,

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.160
you were on your own?
- Yep

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:48.260
- [Interviewer] And what
were you doing then?

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:51.380
- I was prostituting,
I was robbing people,

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:55.470
I was in and out of jails, I
was in and out of institutions,

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:57.433
I was just running rapid,

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:01.430
because it was like, I was on
my own, I had nowhere to go,

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:03.513
I didn't know what to do and I was young.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:06.890
- [Interviewer] And drugs
were part of the picture too?

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:09.430
- They became a part of the
picture when I got down here,

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:12.283
maybe a year and a half, I was almost 18.

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:15.320
I was drinking first,
no I was smoking weed

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:18.313
and then I was drinking and
then graduated to crack.

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:21.470
- [Interviewer] And
you're still using today?

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:24.140
- I still drink.
- [Interviewer] You drink.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:25.110
And do you have kids?

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:27.040
- I have two kids,

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:31.750
which are with their dad
because of my lifestyle,

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:34.020
I was incapable of taking care of kids,

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:37.593
because I was too busy in the
streets, being a street woman.

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:40.230
- [Interviewer] Did you
feel like you're following

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:42.350
in your mom's footsteps?

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:44.473
- A little bit, but I think I was worse.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:48.710
I think I had a lot of
resentments against her,

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:51.823
for telling me I was
lying, for beating on me,

00:04:51.823 --> 00:04:54.150
then I had resentments against my dad,

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:57.580
for in and out of prison and then dying

00:04:57.580 --> 00:04:58.740
and leaving me lonely.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.920
So I feel neglected, lonely, abandoned,

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:05.210
I'm just, I'm everywhere so...

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:08.293
- [Interviewer] Do you think
you have a victim mentality?

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:10.973
- I used to.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:14.700
Now, I think I've took

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:17.123
the role of kind of victimizing people.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:18.853
I'm very

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:23.170
angry,
where I fight a lot,

00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:25.850
like you don't have to say
much and I'll hit you first.

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:27.210
I just,

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:31.593
anything makes me snap, I
don't like people behind me,

00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:35.433
I just, I'm not a people person,

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:39.310
I'm not, cause I don't trust nobody.

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:40.540
- [Interviewer] Where
are you living today?

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:41.577
- In a tent.

00:05:41.577 --> 00:05:42.410
- [Interviewer] On a street right?

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:43.243
- Mhm

00:05:46.373 --> 00:05:48.740
- [Interviewer] Do you still
have contact with your kids?

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:51.100
- I haven't talked to my
daughter in about a year,

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:52.573
my son, in about two years.

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:57.264
- [Interviewer] What's
the hardest thing of all

00:05:57.264 --> 00:05:59.973
your life here on the streets?

00:05:59.973 --> 00:06:02.260
- [Amisha] The hardest thing in my life,

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:03.670
not being around my kids,

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:06.250
feeling like I'm the worst mother ever

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:07.880
and feeling low self-esteem

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:10.670
and low self-worth because I
feel like nobody values me,

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:12.720
so I don't even know how to value myself.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:15.110
- [Interviewer] Nobody seemed to value you

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:16.110
when you were a kid,

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:19.180
so where would you have earned it from?

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:20.130
- [Amisha] Exactly.

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:22.838
- [Interviewer] Do you have friends?

00:06:22.838 --> 00:06:26.005
- [Amisha] I don't believe in friends.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:31.550
- [Interviewer] You ever been in love?

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:32.383
- [Amisha] No.

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:35.360
- [Interviewer] Do you
feel like anybody loved you

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:36.193
as a child?

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:38.617
- [Amisha] My dad.

00:06:38.617 --> 00:06:41.140
- [Interviewer] Your dad did?

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:43.213
- [Amisha] By him being taken so early,

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:47.020
I don't know, now I don't feel like it.

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:53.710
- [Interviewer] What's been
the hardest thing for you

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:54.880
to go through?

00:06:57.481 --> 00:06:59.240
- [Amisha] (sighs) Just being by myself

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:01.243
and wishing my dad was here to help me.

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:12.683
Thank you.

00:07:21.097 --> 00:07:22.680
- [Interviewer] Do you
think maybe you're addicted

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:24.890
to this life, street life?

00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:27.867
- Honestly, I hate this, but
I don't know how to get out.

00:07:36.553 --> 00:07:37.440
- [Interviewer] Do you have
a record that would keep you

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:39.320
from getting work and all that?

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:43.950
- I've been to jail
for sales, DUIs,

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:46.783
so I have a record.

00:07:51.593 --> 00:07:52.600
- [Interviewer] So it's not
just the drug addiction,

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:54.710
it's also--
- Buying and selling.

00:07:54.710 --> 00:07:55.593
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:00.323
How'd you first get introduced to crack?

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:06.330
- My, not my first so-called,
but my second when he...

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:09.600
Actually, I went through a program,

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:12.930
for when I started drinking
and I met him in the program

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:16.100
and he was doing crack and
I was just an alcoholic

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:19.580
so when we did our six
months, we left together

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:22.630
and then we got into it and he was smoking

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:25.827
and that's how it happened, it just...

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:28.550
- [Interviewer] But today
alcohol is your big,

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:29.383
- Yeah

00:08:29.383 --> 00:08:30.380
- [Interviewer] Your main go-to

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:31.280
- That's my first,

00:08:32.434 --> 00:08:35.770
I won't leave alcohol Fernandez,

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:37.300
that's like my best friend right now.

00:08:37.300 --> 00:08:40.100
- [Interviewer] And how do
you, how bad is your alcohol?

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:43.780
- When I wake up, I shake.
- [Interviewer] So your

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:46.369
first thing you do is you start drinking?

00:08:46.369 --> 00:08:48.418
- Mhm.

00:08:48.418 --> 00:08:49.829
- [Interviewer] What's your drink?

00:08:49.829 --> 00:08:52.912
- I drink anything that says liquor.

00:08:58.250 --> 00:08:59.583
- [Interviewer] Do you feel like,

00:09:00.608 --> 00:09:03.333
the rapes that you went through as a kid,

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:07.220
were the most pivotal
thing that happened to you,

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:09.163
as a child?

00:09:10.330 --> 00:09:12.013
I mean that your mom was in and out,

00:09:12.013 --> 00:09:14.040
dad was in and out.
- That

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:18.163
was actually the most traumatic
thing as being six year old,

00:09:18.163 --> 00:09:20.643
besides my mama beating
me, like I was adult,

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:26.683
because I felt like she hated me,

00:09:32.057 --> 00:09:36.320
and I just went with it,
I knew her to hate me

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:38.520
and I never got any answers so...

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:43.683
- [Interviewer] How'd your siblings do?

00:09:45.430 --> 00:09:50.430
- Well, somebody called and
said my mom was doing heroin,

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:53.670
because she used to hang
with the lady next door

00:09:53.670 --> 00:09:56.027
and I can't say if she was or she wasn't,

00:09:56.027 --> 00:09:59.550
but it didn't seem like she was,

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:04.443
so my sister went with her dad's mom

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:08.970
and my brother went with my mama's mom

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:10.693
and then I went with my dad.

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:14.180
So we all kind of like got separated

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:19.930
and then my brother went to a group home

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:23.693
and my sister don't even know who I am,

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:29.713
but I know her because I
used to change her diapers.

00:10:43.853 --> 00:10:44.686
- [Interviewer] Do you feel betrayed

00:10:44.686 --> 00:10:47.470
by everybody you grew up with?

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:48.490
- No, not betrayed.

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:52.730
Just like I was a mistake of being here,

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:54.513
because it seemed like everybody hates me,

00:10:54.513 --> 00:10:57.853
that I try to consider loving.

00:11:07.942 --> 00:11:08.775
- [Interviewer] What's been your darkest,

00:11:08.775 --> 00:11:10.993
what's been your roughest point?

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:14.800
- The roughest point in my life?

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:15.800
The day my dad died.

00:11:20.331 --> 00:11:23.831
It was the summer I graduated high school,

00:11:26.494 --> 00:11:30.277
I graduated July 21st
and he died August 7th.

00:11:52.460 --> 00:11:53.750
- [Interviewer] Do you
even feel like you deserve

00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:54.650
a better life?

00:11:54.650 --> 00:11:56.480
I mean, seeing as he never,
- I don't know.

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:57.313
- [Interviewer] you were never shown

00:11:57.313 --> 00:11:59.480
that you deserve anything?
- I don't know.

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:01.293
- [Interviewer] How far
did you go in school?

00:12:01.293 --> 00:12:02.126
- I graduated 12th grade

00:12:02.126 --> 00:12:07.120
and then I tried
cosmetology, culinary arts

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:13.240
and child development
but I never could finish,

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:17.870
because I was too used
to drinking and smoking

00:12:17.870 --> 00:12:21.270
and I just felt like
school was just taking...

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:23.403
So I just dropped out, I stopped.

00:12:28.146 --> 00:12:28.979
- [Interviewer] Do you feel
like you'll be down here

00:12:28.979 --> 00:12:30.180
the rest of your life?

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:31.013
- Sometimes.

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:33.290
- [Interviewer] You've been
down here your whole life,

00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:34.230
right?

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:35.790
- Just about, I'm 37.

00:12:46.430 --> 00:12:49.010
- [Interviewer] What do you
think would help you the most?

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:49.843
- Right now?

00:12:52.970 --> 00:12:57.330
Somebody that actually loves
me enough to really help me,

00:12:57.330 --> 00:13:00.543
understand me, sometimes
just listen to me,

00:13:00.543 --> 00:13:03.290
I just, I don't know.

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:17.520
Biggest thing that I
want that would help me,

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:20.040
especially spiritually is just somebody

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:23.630
that loves me the same
way that I love them

00:13:23.630 --> 00:13:26.540
and I can't show trust or love to nobody,

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:30.630
because I never get any
so I stay bottled up

00:13:32.170 --> 00:13:33.623
and I stay angry.

00:13:34.750 --> 00:13:37.550
I don't want to be angry,
but down here you have to,

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:40.079
'cause they take advantage
of you, they beat on you,

00:13:40.079 --> 00:13:44.653
they rape you, they drug
you with all kinds of stuff.

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:53.340
- [Interviewer] When
you were a little girl,

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:56.763
did you have dreams of doing
something with your life?

00:13:58.010 --> 00:14:01.340
- I've always wanted
to own my own daycare,

00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:02.683
because I love kids.

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:23.303
- [Interviewer] What do you feel like

00:14:23.303 --> 00:14:26.063
is the most important lesson
you've learned in your life?

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:28.550
You've been through so much.

00:14:33.173 --> 00:14:35.920
- [Amisha] That God doesn't
put more on me than I can bear,

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:37.130
'cause I'm still here.

00:14:50.021 --> 00:14:50.854
- [Interviewer] All right Amisha,

00:14:50.854 --> 00:14:51.687
I'm gonna give you something

00:14:51.687 --> 00:14:54.030
to make your weekend a little easier.

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:56.240
Hopefully you don't spend
it on alcohol.(chuckles)

00:14:56.240 --> 00:14:59.603
- [Amisha] I already have
the alcohol.(giggles)

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:02.577
Thank you.

00:15:02.577 --> 00:15:04.020
- [Interviewer] Thank you.

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:06.068
- [Amisha] Felt good to let
somebody (indistinct) (giggles).

